.SH DESCRIPTION
These commands hold a conversation about code with an AI model through an OpenAI
compatible chat completions API, either OpenAI itself or a local server such as
Ollama, LM Studio or llama.cpp.
.PP
The conversation is the text of the \fC*ai-chat*\fR buffer. Each part starts
with a line holding just \fC## You\fR, \fC## AI\fR or \fC## System\fR, anything
before the first of these is ignored. The whole chat is sent with every
question, so earlier questions and answers may be edited or deleted to change
what the model sees, and the buffer may be saved to a file and the chat carried
on later from any buffer holding these lines. A \fC## System\fR part replaces the
system prompt given by \fB%ai-system-prompt\fR. The buffer uses the markdown
major mode so the code blocks in a reply are hilighted, the hilighting is
worked out from the top of the buffer so the colours do not change as the chat
is scrolled.
.PP
Files are given to the model by attaching them to a question. An attachment is
a single line in a \fC## You\fR part, so the chat stays short:
.CS
@file ~/src/exec.c
@file ~/src/exec.c:2133-2193
@buffer *scratch*
.CE
The first attaches a whole file, the second only lines 2133 to 2193 and the
third a buffer with no file. The text is read when the question is sent, from
the file's buffer when it is loaded, so unsaved changes are included, otherwise
from the disk, and every later question sends it again as it is then. When the
same attachment appears in more than one question its text is only sent with
the last, the earlier ones pointing to it. The model is told each file's name
as the shortest end of its path that tells it apart from the other attached
files, e.g. \fCsrc/exec.c\fR. A file that cannot be read, such as a directory
or a binary file, or a range past its end, is noted in the request and in the
message line, and a range that runs past the end is cut short. Deleting the
line removes the attachment.
.PP
.B ai-chat
shows the \fC*ai-chat*\fR buffer in a popup window and moves to its end, ready
for a question to be written under the last \fC## You\fR line.
.PP
.B ai-send
sends the chat in the current buffer, the last part of which must be a
\fC## You\fR question. The reply is added under a new \fC## AI\fR line, followed
by an empty \fC## You\fR for the next question. Only one request may run at a
time. In the \fC*ai-chat*\fR buffer the following keys are bound:
.IP "\fC\*cc \*cc\fR"
.B ai-send
.IP "\fC\*cc \*ck\fR"
.B ai-cancel
.IP "\fC\*cc i\fR"
.B ai-insert-block
.IP "\fC\*cc n\fR"
.B ai-new
.IP "\fC\*cc m\fR"
.B ai-set-model
.IP "\fC\*cc f\fR"
.B ai-attach
.IP "\fC\*cc v\fR"
.B ai-show-attachment
.IP "\fC\*cc a\fR"
.B ai-apply-edits
.IP "\fC\*cc u\fR"
.B ai-undo-edits
.IP "\fC\*cc e\fR"
.B ai-edit
.LP
.B ai-ask
prompts for a one line question, adds it to the chat and sends it, leaving the
cursor where it was while the reply appears in the chat window.
.PP
.B ai-code-action
sends code from the current buffer with an instruction. The code is the lines of
the active region, or the whole buffer when there is none, and is attached to
the question rather than copied into the chat, together with anything already
attached to a question that has not been written yet. The user is prompted for one of the actions listed in
\fB%ai-actions\fR, with completion, or may type any other question instead. The
actions are listed below. For an action that asks for the complete code, the
model is asked for edit blocks instead when the whole buffer is sent. The
actions are:
.IP \fBexplain\fR
Explain what the code does.
.IP \fBreview\fR
List bugs, edge cases and readability problems, most serious first.
.IP \fBfix\fR
Reply with the corrected code and a list of the changes.
.IP \fBdocument\fR
Reply with the code with documentation comments added.
.IP \fBtests\fR
Write unit tests for the code.
.IP \fBsimplify\fR
Reply with simpler code that behaves the same.
.LP
The text of action \fIname\fR is held in \fB%ai-action-\fIname\fR, so an action
may be changed, or a new one added to \fB%ai-actions\fR with its own variable.
.PP
.B ai-insert-block
copies a fenced code block from the chat. In the chat it uses the block holding
the cursor, or the last block before it; from any other buffer it uses the last
block in \fC*ai-chat*\fR. When the block is an edit block its changes are
applied as by
.BR ai-apply-edits .
Otherwise the user is asked whether to replace the region
last sent by
.B ai-code-action
with it (\fCr\fR, only offered when a region was sent), to insert it at the
cursor of that buffer (\fCi\fR) or just to copy it to the kill buffer and the
system clipboard (\fCc\fR). The choice may instead be given as an argument. A
replacement can be repeated with a later block, as the region is moved to cover
the new code.
.PP
.B ai-attach
adds an attachment to the question being written, starting a new \fC## You\fR
part when the chat does not end in one. In the chat it prompts for files, with
completion, one after another until \fCRETURN\fR is pressed on the directory
that the prompt starts with. In any other buffer it attaches without the line
numbers having to be typed:
.BS
on a line of a \fC*grep*\fR or compile buffer, the lines around that match,
.BU
with an active region, the lines of the region,
.BU
otherwise it asks whether to attach the function around the cursor, found with
the patterns the major mode uses to collapse functions and taking in the
comment above it, or the whole file. Where the mode has none, or the cursor is
not in a function, the lines around the cursor are attached.
.BE
An attachment of a file already attached to the question is merged with any
ranges it overlaps or touches, or replaces them when it is the whole file.
Attaching again is also how a range is moved after the file has been edited.
.PP
.B ai-show-attachment
shows the file or buffer of the attachment line at the cursor in another
window, with the attached lines selected.
.PP
When a chat has attachments the model is asked, by \fB%ai-edit-prompt\fR, to
change a file with edit blocks rather than send all of it back. An edit block
is a fenced block whose info string is \fCedit\fR and the file's name as given
in its attachment, holding one or more changes:
.CS
```edit src/exec.c
<<<<<<< SEARCH
        return mlwrite(MWABORT|MWCLEXEC,(meUByte *)"[Failed to load file %s]", fname);
=======
        return mlwrite(MWABORT|MWCLEXEC,(meUByte *)"[Cannot find %s]", fname);
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```
.CE
.B ai-apply-edits
applies the changes of the reply at or before the cursor, or of the last reply
in \fC*ai-chat*\fR when run from another buffer. The lines of each \fCSEARCH\fR
are looked for in the file, exactly, then ignoring white space at the ends of
lines, then ignoring indentation, in which case the replacement takes on the
file's indentation: tabs for spaces or spaces for tabs a level at a time when
the model used the other, else moved by as many columns as the model's lines
were out. The \fCSEARCH\fR text must be found once, or once within the
attached lines of the file. A file is named as in its attachment, by the end of
its path, which must fit only one attached file, or by a path from where the
attachments' names start; quotes, a leading \fC./\fR and line numbers after
the name are ignored. A block the model left open is closed before the next
block or the end of the reply. Each change is made and shown selected in
another window, and the user is asked whether to keep it: \fCy\fR keeps it,
\fCn\fR puts the old lines back, \fCa\fR keeps it and the rest without
asking and \fCq\fR, or \fCC-g\fR, puts it back and stops. The question warns
when the printf style \fC%\fR formats of the lines have changed, as a reworded
message may then no longer match the arguments of its call. With an argument of
\fC0\fR every change is kept without asking.
.PP
Changing a file that is not attached is only done once the user agrees, and an
empty \fCSEARCH\fR creates the file, if it does not exist, in a new buffer, or
fills an empty file or buffer. A buffer in view mode is not changed. Nothing is
saved. As each change is made, kept or not, a line is added after
its block giving what happened to it, such as
.CS
(edit 1 applied, exec.c line 2163)
.CE
so it is not applied again and the model sees it with the next question;
deleting the line lets that change be applied again. When a kept
change adds or removes lines the line ranges of the file's attachments are
moved to match.
.PP
.B ai-undo-edits
puts back the lines changed by the last
.BR ai-apply-edits ,
and marks each change it undoes as \fCundone\fR. A change whose lines have
been moved by lines added or removed above them is found again, but one whose
lines have been edited since is left, as is a change that only removed lines
once lines have been added or removed anywhere in the file. A file created by an
edit is left in its buffer.
.PP
.B ai-edit
asks for one change to code, such as rewording an error message. Run from a
file, it attaches the file, or the lines of the region when there is one; run
from the chat it uses the files already attached. It then prompts for:
.BS
the text to quote, which starts as the first line of the text last copied, from
the system clipboard or the kill buffer, so a message copied from the code only
needs \fCRETURN\fR. Clearing it leaves the question without a quote.
.BU
how it should change. This is either typed, or the name of a template from
\fB%ai-edit-actions\fR, with completion. A template whose text holds \fC%s\fR
then prompts for the words to put there.
.BE
The question is added to the chat and sent, and once the reply is in, when it
holds edit blocks, they are reviewed as by
.BR ai-apply-edits ,
starting once no key has been pressed for a second so that keys being typed
elsewhere are not taken as answers.
The template \fBmessage\fR rewrites the quoted message as described, keeping
its \fC%\fR formats matching the arguments. For example, after copying a line
that prints \fC[Failed to load file %s]\fR, \fCai-edit\fR, \fCRETURN\fR,
\fCmessage\fR and \fCdrop the word file\fR sends:
.CS
## You
@file ~/microemacs/src/exec.c

```text
return mlwrite(MWABORT|MWCLEXEC,(meUByte *)"[Failed to load file %s]", fname);
```
Rewrite the message quoted above, which comes from the attached code, as
follows: drop the word file. ...
.CE 0
.PP
.B ai-new
clears the chat, asking first if it holds any replies.
.PP
.B ai-cancel
stops a reply that is streaming in, keeping what has arrived so far.
.PP
.B ai-set-model
fetches the list of models from the server and prompts for one, with
completion. If the list cannot be fetched any name may be typed. The model is
saved in the registry, so it is remembered by later sessions; when no model has
been set the first request prompts for one.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The following variables may be set in the user's \fIuser\fR.emf setup file:
.IP \fB%ai-base-url\fR
The API address, \fChttps://api.openai.com/v1\fR by default.
.IP \fB%ai-api-key\fR
The API key. When empty the \fCOPENAI_API_KEY\fR environment variable is used,
which avoids writing the key into a setup file. A local server usually needs no
key.
.IP \fB%ai-model\fR
The model, normally set with
.BR ai-set-model .
.IP \fB%ai-transport\fR
\fCcurl\fR to stream the reply through \fCcurl\fR, \fChttp\fR to use the built in
HTTP client, or empty (the default) to use \fCcurl\fR when it is found on the
path. \fB%ai-curl\fR gives the \fCcurl\fR program, \fCcurl\fR by default.
.IP \fB%ai-system-prompt\fR
The instructions sent before the chat.
.IP \fB%ai-attach-max\fR
When a request with attachments is larger than this many characters, 200000 by
default (about 50000 tokens), the user is asked before it is sent. The size of
each request with attachments is shown in the message line while it is sent.
.IP \fB%ai-attach-lines\fR
The number of lines either side of a match, or of the cursor when there is no
function, that
.B ai-attach
takes, 30 by default.
.IP \fB%ai-edit-actions\fR
The templates offered by
.BR ai-edit ,
\fC|message|\fR by default. The text of template \fIname\fR is held in
\fB%ai-edit-action-\fIname\fR, in which \fC%s\fR is replaced by the user's
words, so a template is added by naming it in the list and setting its
variable, for example:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
set-variable %ai-edit-actions "|message|comment|"
set-variable %ai-edit-action-comment "Add a short comment above the quoted line saying %s."
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
.IP \fB%ai-edit-review\fR
When 1 (the default) the edits of a reply to
.B ai-edit
are reviewed as soon as it is in, set to 0 to review them with
.B ai-apply-edits
instead.
.IP \fB%ai-edit-prompt\fR
The instructions on edit blocks added to the system prompt, or to a \fC## System\fR
part, when the chat has attachments.
.IP \fB%ai-wrap\fR
When 1 (the default) the long prose lines of a reply are split at the buffer's
fill column once it has arrived, leaving code blocks, tables and headings alone.
Set to 0 to keep the reply as sent.
.LP
For example, to use a coding model served by Ollama on the local machine:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
set-variable %ai-base-url "http://localhost:11434/v1"
set-variable %ai-model "qwen2.5-coder"
.CE 0
.Me $ehf-scheme
.SH NOTES
These are macros defined in \fCaichat.emf\fR.
.PP
Nothing is sent unless one of the commands above is run, but everything in the
chat, including the text of every attached file, is then sent to the server.
.PP
The lines of a collapsed part of a buffer are not in the buffer itself, so the
saved file is sent instead, or nothing when the buffer also has unsaved changes,
and an edit to them fails until the part is opened.
.PP
With \fCcurl\fR the reply streams into the chat as it is written and editing can
carry on meanwhile. The API key is passed in a header file readable only by the
user, which is deleted as soon as the reply starts. The built in HTTP client
waits for the whole reply, during which the editor does not respond; it keeps
the key in memory, removing flag \fCd\fR from \fB%http-flags\fR for the request
so the key is not logged in \fC*http-console*\fR. HTTPS through the built in
client needs a version of \*(mS built with OpenSSL.
.PP
A reply line cannot grow past the 65000 character line limit, so a longer one is
broken. A request is built as UTF-8, which needs a version of \*(mS with Unicode
support for characters outside ASCII.
